  Unlocking dagger skill to basic is a great achievement for a monk.
It finally gives them a good ranged weapon.  Throwing early and often
gives  the same effect as multishot, just more slowly.  

  If the monster isn't killed it's damaged.  This is even more important
for monks due to lower melee damage and worse AC (due to lack of body
armor).  Note also that thrown missiles do not break wielded weapon
conduct.

  The point with missile weapons isn't necessarily how many you can
throw at once.  It's how many you can throw before the monster reaches
you.

  A monk throwing +0 daggers early and often at basic skill:  

               Base  Str Dmg  Damage/ Daggers  Ttl    Dmg @ +7
 Str    Range  Dmg    Bonus   Dagger  Thrown   Dmg    Enchant
 ---    -----  ----  -------  ------- -------  ---    --------
 10       5    2.5     +0      2.5       5      12.5    47.5
 16,17    8    2.5     +1      3.5       8      28      84
 18       9    2.5     +2      4.5       9      40     103 
 18/01    9    2.5     +3      5.5       9      50     113
 25      12    2.5     +6      8.5      12     100     184


  That's a little over the top, but only a little.  Early on it's easier
to get the maximum possible distance damage.  Later, at Str:25 and +7
enchantment each dagger will do 19 points of damage.  Even at Str:18
they do 11 damage each.  

  (A light source of range three means 22 damage before the monster is
at melee range.  And Monks get the Eyes of the Overworld.)

  It also understates the case.  Eventually a stack of daggers can be
enchanted to +7.  The last column, "+7 Enchant", gives the total damage
when +7 daggers are thrown starting as early as possible.  Monks
following wielded weapon conduct have no better use for scrolls of
Enchant Weapon.  


  Two important points: 
  - No damage is taken in return.  (Distance is used to replace the 
      missing AC of body armor.)  
  - Wielded weapon conduct is not broken.


   A monk receiving an artifact dagger as a divine gift (Neutral:
Magicbane, Chaotic: Grimtooth, Sting) has indirectly received a great
benefit.




